stratuMAK Structured Text libraries — licensing
===============================================

The files in this directory are

    Copyright (C) 2026 Sascha Ittner <sascha.ittner@modusoft.de>

and are licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or
(at your option) any later version, with the additional permission below.

Why LGPL and not GPL
--------------------

stratuMAK itself is GPL.  These libraries are not, deliberately: a HAL
component that uses one should not be forced to be GPL by that use alone, so
that proprietary modules can be built on the standard function blocks.

Why an added permission is needed anyway
----------------------------------------

The LGPL is written around linking against a library that stays a separate
work.  Nothing here is separate.  A Structured Text library is resolved by
`USING` and its declarations are compiled into the consumer's own module — no
shared object, no ABI, no library boundary at run time.  That is closer to
including a header than to linking, and section 6 of the LGPL, which covers
works containing portions of the library, imposes conditions (supplying object
files so the user can relink) that make no sense for a module whose whole
content is one generated translation unit.

Rather than leave the intent resting on how anyone later reads section 6, it
is written out:

    stratuMAK module exception
    --------------------------
    As a special exception, the copyright holders give you permission to
    compile the Structured Text declarations in this directory into a
    stratuMAK HAL module, and to convey the resulting module under terms of
    your choice, without any of the conditions of section 6 of the LGPL
    applying to that module.

    This exception does not invalidate any other reasons why the module might
    be covered by the GNU General Public License: using a stratuMAK header, a
    GMI interface, or any other GPL part of stratuMAK is unaffected by this
    permission and is governed by its own licence.

    Modifications to the files in this directory are still governed by the
    LGPL: the exception covers using these libraries, not changing them.

In short: use these blocks in whatever module you like; improvements to the
blocks themselves come back.

The full text of the LGPL v2.1 is at
<https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html>.

It is not yet bundled: the tree ships COPYING (GPL v2) and COPYING.more, and a
COPYING.LIB should be added beside them before these libraries are distributed.
